<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Lu'lu'id dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1233 CE – 1262 CE · capital Mosul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The rule of Badr al-Din Lu'lu', a former slave who became master of Mosul, and of his son, ended by the Mongol sack of the city in 1262.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu">Badr al-Din Lu'lu'</a> was a mamluk, probably of Armenian origin, in the household of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> Arslan Shah of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zengid-dynasty-of-mosul">Zengid dynasty of Mosul</a>. As regent for the last young <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zengid-dynasty">Zengids</a> he held effective power from the 1210s, and in 1233 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> recognised him as ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in his own right.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu">Lu'lu'</a> was a shrewd survivor, balancing between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of Rum and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, to whom he submitted after 1243. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> under his rule was celebrated for inlaid brass metalwork and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> painting, and he built shrines, including ones honouring Shia figures, in the city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu">Lu'lu'</a> died in 1259, his son al-<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/salihids">Salih</a> Isma'il rebelled against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu's</a> forces besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, which fell in 1262, and al-Salih was executed. Mosul then came under direct Ilkhanid rule.</p></div><aside class